    "created_at": "2022-02-07T16:14:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-02-07T16:14:48Z",
    "body": "I think adding adding [EnforceRange] would make sense.  The potential for compat issues alluded to by @domenic is quite low given that any app that depends on successfully allocating ImageBitmaps with sizes in the range that the modulo conversion renders usable is probably misbehaving in the first place.  And the fact the Chromium throws exceptions (although they are not mandated by the spec) for ImageBitmaps that are too large to be allocated means that interoperability is already broken in this area.  Therefore, I am not concerned about making compat any worse here.  If anything, we have an opportunity to fix this issue and improve compatibility at the same time by properly spec'ing storage size overflow behavior.  In Chromium we generally treat overflows in buffer size arithmetic the same as we treat allocation failures, whenever there are provisions in the spec for handling allocation failures.  In the family of canvas-related APIs (including ImageBitmap), there are no provisions in the spec for internal out-of-memory errors.\r\n\r\nSo, in addition to overflows, the general handling of allocation failures is under-specified and is something we could address. If I am not mistaken, the only strictly compliant behavior for handling internal allocation failures that are not explicitly covered by the standard is to crash the browser with an out-of-memory exception.  Implementations have deviated from this, especially for APIs that tend allocate large buffers internally (e.g. canvas).  There is a tacit consensus among implementors that failing silently is preferable to crashing. So implementations are mostly interoperable in their non-compliant handling of OOM errors (creatImageBitmap being an exception).\r\n\r\nFirefox's behavior, as explained by Kaiido@ above, goes along with the principle of best effort / silent failure.  AFAIK, the main argument for failing silently is that it avoids breaking web pages by throwing unspec'ed (and therefore unexpected) exceptions.  However, for promise-based APIs, such as createImageBitmap, unexpected rejections are not as disruptive as unexpected throws. I concede that is debatable, but nonetheless, Chromium's implementation rejects ImageBitmap promises on OOM.  We should fix that inter-op issue regardless of what happens with this bug.  With the spec's status quo, Chromium's behavior should be changed to match Firefox's IMHO.  I filed a bug against Chromium for this: crbug.com/1294833\r\n",
