    "created_at": "2022-02-08T15:55:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-02-08T15:55:27Z",
    "body": "For canvases, the silent failures work well because they allow the synchronous APIs to be implemented asynchronously under the hood.  For example, if an exception had to be thrown when a canvas is resized to dimensions that cannot be allocated, that would force the implementation to be synchronous, which would be quite bad for performance. With createImageBitmap, we are dealing with an API that is async.  So the silent failure approach loses much of its appeal.  By rejecting promises, we provide actionable feedback to devs and allow apps to handle and workaround the failure.\r\n\r\nEarlier in this thread, I was leaning in favor of silent failures. Now I realize that doing silent failures just because that's what we've always done in canvas-related APIs is not really that good of a reason.   Consistency has its merits, but there are other arguments too.",
